# Section 4, Element Types / Hex and Pie Segmented Mirrors: 3.2's NEW dialog for a 7-segment Pie
# source (Aperture 3.65, nGridPts 31, nSeg 7, width 1.2, gap 0, the seven SegCoord triples, SegXgrid),
# then the segment elements s1, s2 and s7 (Segment / Conic, f 1.46, e 1, psi 0,0,-1, zElt 1.46; s2..s7
# ask only for their rotation points).  Dave 2026-10-19: Section 4's NEW tutorials become plain
# generated dialogs.  The answers are 3.2's.  Four slices, as 3.2 printed four pieces: the grid-type
# menu to nGridPts (_1; 3.2 then shows the Figure 23 coordinate chart), xGrid to SegXgrid (_2), elements
# 1-2 (_3) and element 7 (_4).  Setup (not printed): the source's chief ray, wavelength and the
# elements 3-6 and 8-10 as examples/SegDemo.in (Appendix A.5): ChfRayDir 0,0,-1 at z=1.5, zSource 1d19,
# Wavelen 1d-4; s3..s6 at SegDemo's rotation points; SecMir / RefSurf / FclPlane.
# Against 3.2's dialog: the type menus list more types; the source asks for base units (none); the
# Pie grid asks no obscuration ratio (Obscratn stays 0; SegDemo.in has 0.3); each Segment now asks
# 'Enter glass type (FixedIndex)' before the index (answered with its default); the principal-axis
# prompt now defaults to +z (the chief ray's reverse); the echo adds nCoat.
# NOT traced (a finding for Dave): 3.2's psi answer 0,0,-1 is the reverse of SegDemo.in's psiElt
# 0,0,1, while its rotation points (z=+0.2569563356 = 1.225^2/(4*1.46)) lie on the +z-sagging parent
# that psi 0,0,1 describes.  With 3.2's answers the deck loses 584 of 649 rays at elements 8-9 (surface
# miss); with psi 0,0,1 the same deck traces as SegDemo.in does (RMS OPD 7e-12 at element 9).  The
# pins are the dialog's values only.
